Sculpt and rig the **Caldera Slag**, a lumbering molten elemental — a hunched mass of
glowing magma sheathed in a cracked cooling-basalt crust — as a {{voxel.width}}x{{voxel.height}}x{{voxel.depth}} **skinned**
character using only the `sn-skin` tool. You build it as **one continuous skin**: a
single whole-body signed-distance field composited from CSG primitives, bound to a
skeleton you invent, and you **author its animations** as F-curves so the whole body
deforms across its joints.

This test measures **creativity and craft**, not instruction-following: the brief tells
you *what the Slag is* and *how it must move*, but the forms you composite, the bones
and joints you lay out, and how you break the motion up are **entirely yours to invent**.
Work out the skeleton a hunched, lumbering creature needs.

## How sculpting and rigging works

The only way to sculpt the field and edit the rig is the `sn-skin` binary already on
your `PATH`. You build the model by calling it **one operation at a time**, and the
ordered operations you issue — recorded to `actions.json` — together with the skeleton
and animations you define are your submission.

A skinned character is **one whole-body field / one continuous mesh** — there is **no
`--part` flag**. You composite the whole body with the CSG primitives (`add-sphere` /
`add-box` / `add-ellipsoid` / `add-cylinder` and their `subtract-*` counterparts,
softened into one organic mass with `--blend`); the field starts **empty**; colors are
**opaque** `#rrggbb` (no alpha). Each call only records the operation and renders
**nothing** — run `sn-skin render` to (re)draw the whole-model preview (`--view
iso|front|side|top` for a camera, `--time <ms> --animation <name>` for a preview posed
with actual skin deformation), and you **must** run `sn-skin render` before you finish
so it emits the skinned `mesh.glb` geometry and the derived skin weights the run is
scored from — an unrendered model is scored as empty.

**You invent the rig.** Define the bones your creature needs with `define-bone` (each a
head→tail segment under a parent), position them with `set-bone`, place degrees of
freedom on them with `define-joint`, and author each required animation with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. The per-vertex skin weights that bind the mesh to the
skeleton are **derived automatically** at render (bone-heat diffusion, up to four
influences per vertex) — you do not paint them by hand.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, for example:

```
sn-skin add-ellipsoid --x 3 --y 3 --z 3 --rx 5 --ry 4 --rz 5 --color "#17151a"
sn-skin define-bone --name pelvis --parent ""
```

(The values above are just an example — shape your own body and skeleton however you
like.) Run `sn-skin --help` to list every operation **and** the skeleton and animation
subcommands (defining and positioning bones, placing joints, and authoring animations
with `define-animation`/`add-keyframe`), and `sn-skin <operation> --help` for one
operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract. **Read
`model.png` after you run `sn-skin render`** to see the whole creature's progress, and
use `sn-skin render --time <ms> --animation <name>` to see the skin **actually deform**
at an instant of an animation — that posed preview is how you confirm the body flexes
across its joints rather than tearing.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Composite and rig the Slag up operation by operation until `model.png` matches the
brief and the rig is complete: one continuous skin — a hunched molten mass sheathed in
cracked basalt crust — bound to a skeleton you invented. **Author all three required
animations** as F-curves with the `sn-skin` animation subcommands, each deforming the
whole body across its joints:

- **`advance`** — a game-triggered playable that **loops**: a lumbering walk that hauls
  the mass forward on planted limbs while the pelvis, spine, and crust flex across the
  joints, easing so a heavy body carries weight rather than sliding linearly.
- **`slam`** — a game-triggered playable that **plays once and holds**: the creature
  rears and brings its mass down, the impact carrying up through the shoulders and spine
  as continuous-skin deformation, then settles.
- **`emerge`** — a game-triggered playable that **plays once and holds**: the body
  heaves up out of the ground into its standing pose, the skin stretching into shape
  across the joints as it rises.

The animations are the fixed contract — produce all three, by these names, each
actually deforming the skin; the bones and joints that realize them are yours. Run
`sn-skin render` before you finish so the geometry and skin weights are emitted. When
you are satisfied, stop: the recorded operation log and `rig.json` are your finished
submission.
